1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,634
Marrow helps you trace a conclusion back to its evidence.

2
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:11,009
Postmortem Court opens with a checkout outage. The database pool is full.

3
00:00:12,600 --> 00:00:17,779
Submit the case. Marrow labels the source material so each examiner can cite it.

4
00:00:20,600 --> 00:00:26,814
In round two, each examiner challenges a colleague. Their citations point back to the same exhibits.

5
00:00:29,700 --> 00:00:34,064
The panel traces the outage to a cancelled order that left a database connection open.

6
00:00:37,300 --> 00:00:43,064
Click a graph node to inspect the evidence. Export the report to keep the findings and transcript.

7
00:00:45,200 --> 00:00:51,108
Now open Defense Tribunal. A research team claims its new search method gives more accurate answers.

8
00:00:53,700 --> 00:00:58,139
The same engine labels manuscript passages and seats three academic reviewers.

9
00:01:01,400 --> 00:01:07,753
Their question is different. Does the new method win because it is better, or because it gets more computing time?

10
00:01:11,300 --> 00:01:17,233
The editor asks for a fair comparison. The decision letter lists the blocking flaws and the experiments needed.

11
00:01:19,200 --> 00:01:25,579
Both apps share the evidence graph, review engine and storage. Each supplies its own questions and report.
